= Mint

#emph[Scope: This entry treats the #emph[Mentha] genus (Lamiaceae) as a unit, with explicit attention to species distinctions where they matter for classical attestation and clinical safety. The principal species: #emph[Mentha × piperita] L. (peppermint — a sterile hybrid of M. aquatica × M. spicata, the principal modern medicinal mint, very high in menthol); #emph[M. spicata] L. (spearmint — the principal culinary mint, lower in menthol, sweeter); #emph[M. arvensis] L. (corn mint / Japanese mint / Mentha canadensis — the industrial menthol source); #emph[M. aquatica] L. (water mint); #emph[M. longifolia] (L.) Huds. (horse mint); #emph[M. pulegium] L. (pennyroyal — #emph[a distinct mint with substantial toxic potential: pulegone hepatotoxicity and abortifacient use have caused multiple fatal cases]; the dietary uses of M. piperita and M. spicata are #emph[not] safe references for pennyroyal). Classical antiquity did not consistently distinguish among Mentha species — the Greek μίνθη, Latin #emph[mentha], Arabic نعناع, Chinese 薄荷, and Sanskrit / Hindi #emph[pudīnā] all refer to multiple species in regional usage. Status: classical in all four canonical traditions, with particularly deep Tibb (Avicennan canon and Levantine daily use) and Chinese (Bencao Gangmu surface-releasing-aromatic) attestation; Greco-Roman with rich mythological register (the #emph[Mintha] nymph myth); Ayurvedic-classical attestation is #emph[thinner and contested in the saṃhitā literature], with the modern Indian #emph[pudīnā] register being post-classical Persian-Mughal-Indian elaboration; New Testament attestation (Matthew 23:23, Luke 11:42 — the "mint, dill, cumin" tithing passage); Mishnaic-rabbinic attestation in Jewish ritual herb-tithing register. Modern register: #emph[peppermint oil for irritable bowel syndrome] is one of the better-supported modern herbal evidence-based clinical applications.]

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* The genus _Mentha_ L. (Lamiaceae / Labiatae) contains ~25 species and many hybrids, with the taxonomy *genuinely difficult* — many natural hybrids, frequent sterility, regional variation. Principal cultivated and medicinal species:
- *_Mentha × piperita_ L.* — *peppermint*; a *sterile hybrid* of _M. aquatica_ × _M. spicata_; the principal modern medicinal mint; very high in *menthol* (35–55% of essential oil) and *menthone*; the hybrid origin was recognized in the late 18th c. (John Ray, then established) and the species was *only created horticulturally* in 17th-c. England.
- *_Mentha spicata_ L.* — *spearmint*; lower in menthol (~0.5–1%), higher in *carvone* (the principal flavor compound — also found in caraway); the principal culinary mint of Mediterranean, Levantine, and Anglo-American cuisines.
- *_Mentha arvensis_ L.* / *_M. canadensis_ L. f.* — *corn mint*, *Japanese mint*; the principal *industrial menthol crystals source* (the East Asian and Brazilian commercial menthol production largely from this species).
- *_Mentha pulegium_ L.* — *pennyroyal*; *high in pulegone (60–80% of oil)*; *toxic* — see safety section; classical use as abortifacient and flea-repellent (the Latin _pulex_ "flea" gives the species name).
- *_Mentha aquatica_ L.* — *water mint*; the wet-soil wild species; parent of peppermint.
- *_Mentha longifolia_ (L.) Huds.* — *horse mint*; broadly distributed wild mint; the classical Mediterranean and Levantine wild mint.
- *_Mentha suaveolens_ Ehrh.* — *apple mint*, *pineapple mint*.

The genus name *Mentha* (Latin) from Greek *μίνθη (mínthē)* / *μίνθα (míntha)* — *the eponymous nymph of Greek mythology*: *Mintha (Μίνθη)*, a *Naiad* daughter of the river god Cocytus (one of the rivers of the underworld, "wailing"), was the *mistress of Hades*; *Persephone* (Hades' wife, daughter of Demeter) in jealousy *transformed her into the mint plant* (or, in some versions, *trampled her into the earth* whence the mint grew). *Mount Mintha* (Μίνθη) in Elis (western Peloponnese, near Pylos) preserves the nymph's name as a real geographical feature. The myth is preserved by *Strabo* (_Geography_ 8.3.14), *Ovid* (_Metamorphoses_ 10.728–730), and *Servius* (commentary on Virgil's _Aeneid_). The Greek-Latin name is *one of the great mythologically-anchored botanical etymologies* — comparable to *narcissus*, *hyacinth*, *cypress*, and *daphne* in preserving an Olympian transformation myth in the plant's name to the present.#footnote[Etymology and mythology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _mentha_); Beekes (s.v. μίνθη); Strabo, _Geographica_ 8.3.14; Ovid, _Metamorphoses_ 10.728–730; Servius on Virgil. Mount Mintha in Elis: standard classical geography. The Mintha → mint transformation is one of the canonical Olympian botanical metamorphoses preserved in the modern English plant name.]

*English:* *Mint*, from Old English _minte_, from Latin _mentha_, from Greek μίνθη. Continuously preserved across Germanic languages: German _Minze_, Dutch _munt_, Danish _mynte_. *Peppermint* — Modern English compound, 17th c., for _M. × piperita_; *spearmint* — Modern English, 16th c., for _M. spicata_, referring to the spike-shaped (spica) inflorescence; *pennyroyal* — Modern English, from Old French _puliol-royal_ "royal pulegium," a folk-etymology of the Latin species name.

*Greek (classical and biblical):* *μίνθη (mínthē)* — classical Greek; *ἡδύοσμον (hēdyosmon)* — "sweet-smelling" — the New Testament Greek for mint, used in the canonical *Matthew 23:23* and *Luke 11:42* passages: "*you tithe mint (hēdyosmon) and dill and cumin and have neglected the weightier matters of the law*" — Jesus's critique of pharisaical tithing practice. The passage is *the canonical biblical-Christian attestation* of mint as one of the Levantine ritual-tithing herbs in 1st-c. Jewish religious practice. The use of *hēdyosmon* (rather than the more general _mínthē_) suggests *spearmint or related sweet-flavored mint* rather than the more pungent species.#footnote[Matthew 23:23, Luke 11:42 — Greek New Testament; standard biblical commentaries on the herb-tithing passage; Zohary, _Plants of the Bible_, on mint identification. The Levantine 1st-c. herb-tithing context is significant: mint, dill, and cumin were the kitchen-garden herbs subject to maʿaser sheni / second-tithe under Pharisaic practice — the very minor things over which the legalistic preoccupation is criticized.]

*Hebrew (Mishnaic, post-biblical):* *מִינְתָּה (mintha)* — Mishnaic-rabbinic Hebrew for mint, from the Greek; preserved in Talmudic agricultural and dietary discussions. Modern Hebrew *מנטה (menta)*. The Hebrew Bible itself does *not* contain a direct mint reference; the Mishnaic-Talmudic register and the Greek New Testament Matthew 23:23 are the principal Jewish-Christian classical attestations.

*Chinese:* *薄荷 (bòhé)* — the canonical Chinese drug name; characters *薄 bò* "thin" + *荷 hé* (the lotus character, used here apparently for sound) — possibly originally a *phonetic-loan rendering of a Sanskrit/Persian original*, suggesting Han-Tang Silk-Road introduction. Although Chinese mint cultivation extends to pre-Tang antiquity (with some classical authors discussing it), the drug *薄荷* enters the major Bencao tradition formally in the *Tang Bencao* (新修本草, 唐本草, 659) and is extensively treated in the *Bencao Gangmu*. The Chinese 薄荷 most commonly refers to *Mentha haplocalyx* (= Chinese mint, the canonical Bencao species) — a distinct species from European peppermint, but functionally and pharmacologically very similar.#footnote[薄荷 in _Tang Bencao_ (新修本草, 659); _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578 — substantial entry; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; modern Chinese pharmacopoeia. The phonetic-loan character analysis is contested; some Chinese etymologists treat 薄荷 as a Chinese descriptive coinage.]

*Sanskrit:* The classical Sanskrit attestation of mint is *thin and contested in the saṃhitā literature*. Possible classical Sanskrit names: *फूतकरी (phūtkarī)*, *अजगन्धा (ajagandhā)* — though both identifications are debated and may refer to other Lamiaceae. The Hindi *पुदीना (pudīnā)* is a *Persian borrowing* (from Persian پودینه _pūdīna_) that entered Indian register principally via Mughal-Islamic transmission (15th–18th c.); *pudīnā is not a classical Sanskrit name in the Vedic / saṃhitā register*. Some modern Ayurvedic literature has proposed Sanskrit calques (*पूतिहा pūtihā*, *रोचिनी rocinī*) but these are *modern coinages or post-classical commentarial additions*. *The classical Indian Lamiaceae focus is on तुलसी (tulsi, holy basil — Ocimum sanctum / O. tenuiflorum)*, which has very rich Vedic and saṃhitā attestation; mint by comparison is a *post-classical or thin-classical* Indian herb. Modern Indian Ayurvedic clinical and dietetic use of *pudīnā* is rich and developed, but the *classical canonical attestation should be honestly flagged as modest*.#footnote[Sanskrit / Hindi mint nomenclature: Monier-Williams (s.v. _phūtkarī_, _ajagandhā_ — both with uncertain identification); standard Persian → Hindi etymology for _pudīnā_ (from Pers. پودینه, of uncertain ultimate origin). The classical Indian Lamiaceae register: see eventual `tulsi.typ` — tulsi is the canonical Indian holy basil with extensive Vedic / Purāṇic / Ayurvedic attestation; mint is a much thinner Indian classical referent.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb register):* *نَعنَاع (naʿnāʿ)* — the canonical Arabic / Tibb name; treated by Avicenna in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a major drug; central in Levantine, Maghrebi, Persian, and South Asian Muslim cuisine and pharmacy. Persian *نعنا (naʿnā)*; Urdu *نعناع (naʿnāʿ)* / *पुदीना (pudīnā)*. The Tibb register on naʿnāʿ is *substantively developed*: it is one of the *most-consumed Tibb daily-aromatic-tonic drugs* across the Arab-Muslim world.

*Maghrebi mint tea — historical note:* The famous *Moroccan / Tunisian / Maghrebi mint tea (شاي بالنعناع / shāy bi-l-naʿnāʿ — "tea with mint")* — Chinese green tea brewed with fresh mint and abundant sugar — is *not classical*; it is a *19th-century cultural construct*. Green tea reached Morocco only in the *mid-19th c.* (the most-cited account attributes the introduction to British trade during the Crimean blockade of Russian markets, with British tea-traders redirecting cargo to Maghrebi ports), and the *mint-with-green-tea pairing* was a *Berber and broader Moroccan adaptation* with no Avicennan or earlier Tibb precedent. The Moroccan mint-tea ceremony is now a *defining cultural icon*, but the *classical Tibb mint register is not the Moroccan tea register* — the older Tibb register uses *mint infusion alone, mint in compound preparations, mint with rose-water, mint with vinegar* (the canonical Avicennan combinations).#footnote[Maghrebi mint tea history: standard historical literature on the global tea trade; Ellis, _The Empire of Tea_ and similar works; modern Moroccan cultural-history scholarship. The 19th-c. introduction is well-documented; the pairing's status as a *cultural icon* of timeless tradition is itself a 20th-c. cultural-historical phenomenon (often deconstructed in modern Moroccan-studies literature). The classical Tibb mint preparations are older and distinct.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*薄荷 (bòhé)* is *classical in Chinese medicine*, with formal Bencao entry from the *Tang Bencao* (659) onwards and extensive use in surface-releasing and Liver-clearing formulas. The Chinese clinical register is *cool-pungent-aromatic surface-releasing*.#footnote[薄荷 entries: _Tang Bencao_ (新修本草, 659); _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Cool (涼).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Pungent (辛 xīn).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Liver (肝).

*Actions & indications:*
- *疏散風熱 (shū sàn fēng rè)* — *disperses wind-heat* — the cardinal action; for *wind-heat external invasion* — fever, sore throat, headache, red eyes, mild cough. 薄荷 is the canonical *cool surface-releasing aromatic* of Chinese medicine, paired with cool herbs for wind-heat patterns (rather than the warming surface-releasing herbs like ephedra or cinnamon for wind-cold).
- *清利頭目 (qīng lì tóu mù)* — *clears and benefits the head and eyes* — for headache, red eyes, blurred vision, sore throat from wind-heat or Liver-qi rising.
- *疏肝行氣 / 透疹 (shū gān xíng qì / tòu zhěn)* — *soothes Liver and moves qi / expresses rashes* — for Liver-qi constraint (especially the *premenstrual emotional-and-physical syndrome*) and for the *eruption of measles, rash, or skin lesions that need to be drawn out* — the classical Chinese "expressing rashes" register.
- *Decocting note:* 薄荷 contains *volatile aromatic compounds* (menthol-class) that *evaporate during long cooking*; classical Chinese decoction technique calls for *adding 薄荷 only in the last 5 minutes* (*後下* hòu xià, "added later") to preserve the aromatic action.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity with internal heat (the dispersing-aromatic action can deplete yin); spontaneous sweating from qi vacuity (the dispersing action is contraindicated); pregnancy at high therapeutic doses; nursing mothers (the herb is reported to *reduce lactation* — relevant for those seeking to wean).#footnote[Actions, cautions, and decocting technique: 薄荷 in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; standard modern Chinese pharmacology texts. The lactation-reduction note is preserved in classical and modern Chinese clinical observation.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

The classical Sanskrit attestation of mint is *thin and contested*; the modern Indian *pudīnā* register is well-developed but largely *post-classical Persian-Mughal elaboration* rather than canonical saṃhitā material. Reading mint by extension and per the modern Indian commentarial dravyaguna register:

*Rasa (taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild) — the standard reading for an aromatic Lamiaceae.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry) when fresh-aromatic; Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp / penetrating), Sugandhi (सुगन्धि, fragrant).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) per the standard modern Indian Ayurvedic placement — though the *sensory cooling effect* of menthol-rich mint creates an interpretive complexity: the *vipāka-and-virya* heating effect coexists with the *immediate sensory cooling*. Modern dravyaguna commentators have given various readings; some assign *anuṣṇa-aśīta* (neither heating nor cooling) for peppermint specifically. The placement is interpretive — flagged.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* The *immediate-cooling-with-warming-vīrya* paradox — the sensory cold-without-temperature-cold phenomenon mediated by menthol's *TRPM8 cold-receptor agonism* (modern pharmacology) — is the kind of *not-directly-predictable* effect that could warrant a prabhāva assignment in classical reasoning; modern dravyaguna commentators sometimes elevate it to prabhāva level. The classical saṃhitā does not directly address this — flagged as interpretive.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Kapha and Vāta* (the dispersing-aromatic profile); aggravates *Pitta* in heavy use (the warming vīrya).

*Karma (by extension / modern Indian commentarial):*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — kindles digestion, supports _āma_-digestion.
- _Vāta-anulomana_ — relieves Vāta downward.
- _Mukha-vāsa_ — mouth-freshener (modern Indian usage).
- _Hṛdya_ — cardiotonic, mild.
- _Kāsahara_ — anti-cough, mild.
- _Jvarahara_ — anti-fever (mild).
- _Garbhāśaya-uttejaka_ — mild uterine-stimulant (relevant to pregnancy caution).
- _Stanya-nāśana_ — modestly *anti-lactating* — converging with the Chinese reading and modern phytopharmacology.
- *Used in:* indigestion, gas, nausea, headache, cold-cough (with cooling-pungent register), mouth and breath hygiene; with caution in pregnancy and lactation.

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

- *(S, extended) Conditional viruddha:* Pitta excess; _amlapitta_; ulcerative or inflammatory conditions of the digestive tract.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Gastroesophageal reflux* — peppermint can worsen GERD by relaxing the lower esophageal sphincter; *patients with reflux should avoid concentrated peppermint preparations* — a modern caveat reasonably well-supported.
  - *Pregnancy* — therapeutic-dose use cautioned; culinary use generally safe.
  - *Nursing mothers seeking to maintain lactation* — peppermint and spearmint may reduce milk supply.
  - *Pennyroyal toxicity* — *_Mentha pulegium_ (pennyroyal) is hepatotoxic and abortifacient via pulegone metabolism to menthofuran and other reactive intermediates*; *multiple fatal cases* from abortifacient use are documented (notably the 1978 Colorado case of Margaret Hill and others); *pennyroyal essential oil is one of the most dangerous of all common essential oils*. *Pennyroyal must not be confused with peppermint or spearmint in safety contexts*.
  - *Drug interactions* — peppermint inhibits CYP3A4 modestly; clinical interactions modest.#footnote[Pennyroyal toxicity: extensive forensic and clinical literature; the pulegone → menthofuran hepatotoxic mechanism is well-characterized; the historical abortifacient use has caused documented fatalities. Peppermint and spearmint are *not* substantially toxic at culinary or normal therapeutic doses; the pennyroyal caveat is species-specific and must be flagged.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*Naʿnāʿ (نَعنَاع)* is *fully classical and substantively developed in Tibb* — Avicenna treats it in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a substantial drug; Ibn al-Bayṭār treats it extensively; the Levantine, Maghrebi, Persian, and South Asian Muslim culinary and pharmaceutical traditions preserve continuous daily use. The Tibb register is *warming-aromatic-digestive-cardiotonic*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Mizāj:* *Hot in the 2nd degree, Dry in the 2nd degree* per Avicenna (_al-Qānūn_ II, _naʿnāʿ_) — the standard placement for a strong warming-aromatic Lamiaceae; converges with the modern Indian Ayurvedic uṣṇa reading; *divergent from the Chinese 涼 (cool) reading* — a cross-system divergence reflecting different clinical-use emphases and possibly different cultivar / chemotype focus. The Tibb classification anchors the *winter-warming and digestive-stomachic* use; the Chinese cool classification anchors the *wind-heat-clearing surface-releasing* use.

*Afʿāl:*
- *Muqawwī al-maʿida* — *stomach-tonic* — the cardinal action; for cold-pattern dyspepsia, nausea, vomiting, poor appetite.
- *Kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — *carminative* — for flatulence and gas.
- *Muqawwī al-qalb* — cardiotonic; *muqawwī al-dimāgh* — brain-tonic.
- *Mufarriḥ* — mood-elevating; daily *naʿnāʿ* infusion is taken as a *mood-elevating warming-aromatic tonic*.
- *Munaḍḍif li-l-ṣadr* — chest-cleansing (mild).
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* — mild diuretic.
- *Muqawwī al-bāh* — mild aphrodisiac in some classical readings.
- *Yaʿqil al-tabīʿa* — modest constipating action (modulating role on bowel movement).
- *Mufattiḥ* — mild deobstruent.
- *Muḍādd al-ʿufūnah* — anti-putrefactive (the antimicrobial register; modern carvone and menthol antimicrobial pharmacology validates).

*Istemālāt:* cold-pattern dyspepsia and digestive complaints; nausea (especially during travel — the canonical *travel-and-motion-sickness* preventive use, preserved across the Tibb world); cold-pattern headache; melancholy and low mood; chest congestion; mild fever; oral hygiene; topical use for skin and joint complaints.

*Mawāneʿ:* hot-mizāj patients in excess; pregnancy at therapeutic doses; lactating women (the lactation-reducing register); patients with peptic ulcer or severe reflux.

*Muṣliḥ:* rose-water (_māʾ al-ward_); sugar / honey; lemon — the latter especially in the *naʿnāʿ-and-laymūn* (mint-lemon) combination, which is the canonical Levantine and Maghrebi *summer cooling beverage* that paradoxically uses a Hot-Dry mizāj herb to *cool* (via the sensory-cooling and digestive-supportive register, balanced by the cooling-sour lemon).

*Badal:* *fūdhanj* (پودینه — Persian, related to pennyroyal in some classical sources; care needed for species identification); *raiḥān* (basil, in some preparations); *zaʿtar* (oregano) for the digestive-stomachic register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Mā' al-naʿnāʿ (ماء النعناع) — mint water / mint distillate* — the canonical Tibb preparation: mint leaves distilled to a clear water; taken as digestive tonic, for nausea, motion sickness, mild fever. The *Maghrebi and Levantine pharmacy still produces classical naʿnāʿ distillate*.
- *Sharbat-i Naʿnāʿ* — mint syrup, with sugar / honey and sometimes lemon; the canonical Tibb summer-and-winter beverage.
- *Compound digestive electuaries (Maʿjūn)* — naʿnāʿ as canonical aromatic in dozens of compound digestive preparations.
- *Tabbūleh / fattūsh (تبولة / فتوش)* — the Levantine herb-and-bread / herb-and-bulgur salads; *raw fresh mint* is *constitutive*, paired with parsley, tomato, lemon, sumac, olive oil. These are *daily Levantine dietary use*, with strong dietetic-medical register: cooling-aromatic-digestive-blood-purifying.
- *Yogurt-and-mint preparations* — yogurt with chopped mint as the cooling Levantine summer side; the *fatteh* and *ayran* register.
- *Tea preparations (regional, classical and post-classical)*:
  - *Maghrebi mint tea* — green tea + fresh mint + sugar; *19th-c. development*, now culturally central but not classical Tibb.
  - *Egyptian and Levantine naʿnāʿ infusion (شاي نعناع)* — plain mint infusion in hot water, with sugar; *the classical Tibb mint preparation, distinct from the Maghrebi-tea form*.
  - *Iranian and Turkish mint tea* — variations.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *No direct major canonical hadith provenance for naʿnāʿ specifically*. Some narrations include mint in dietary contexts and as a household herb of the Prophet's household, but no specific canonical hadith elevates naʿnāʿ to the prophetic-medicine status of black seed, dates, honey, or olive oil. The herb's classical Tibb status is *Avicennan / Galenic-Arabic with rich cultural register*, but the *Qurʾānic / Tibb al-Nabawī register is mild*.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _naʿnāʿ_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _naʿnāʿ_ and related; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III; Levantine and Maghrebi culinary-dietetic literature. The cross-system mizāj divergence (Tibb hot, Chinese cool) is real and reflects different clinical-use focus.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *銀翹散 (Yín Qiào Sǎn, "Honeysuckle and Forsythia Powder")* — *the canonical wind-heat releasing formula*; 金銀花 + 連翹 + 桔梗 + 薄荷 + 竹葉 + 甘草 + 荊芥穗 + 淡豆豉 + 牛蒡子 + 蘆根. From Wu Jutong's _Wen Bing Tiao Bian_ (Qing dynasty, 1798); *one of the most-prescribed Chinese formulas globally* for wind-heat (early-stage febrile illness with sore throat and head-and-eye signs). 薄荷 is constitutive.
- *桑菊飲 (Sāng Jú Yǐn, "Mulberry-Chrysanthemum Drink")* — _Wen Bing Tiao Bian_; for wind-heat with cough; 薄荷 is a key ingredient.
- *逍遙散 (Xiāo Yáo Sǎn, "Free and Easy Wanderer Powder")* — *one of the most-prescribed Chinese formulas globally* (also covered in `dang-gui.typ`); 薄荷 is a *modifying ingredient added last* (per the late-addition technique) — its inclusion is what makes the formula *Free and Easy* in the most-complete reading (with Liver-soothing being the central function).
- *加味逍遙散 / 丹梔逍遙散* — variants of Xiao Yao San with additional Liver-clearing herbs.

=== Ayurveda

- *Pudina cūrṇa* — the powder; for digestive complaints, gas, mild nausea.
- *Pudina arka* — distillate; modern Ayurvedic preparation for digestion, headache, oral hygiene.
- *Pudina chutney* — the canonical Indian condiment: fresh mint + cilantro + green chili + ginger + lemon + cumin + salt; daily Indian culinary-dietetic preparation; carminative and digestive.
- *Pudina raita* — mint yogurt; cooling summer side.
- *Pudina paratha* — mint-stuffed flatbread.
- Modern Indian Ayurvedic pharmacy includes mint in numerous compound preparations as a digestive-aromatic adjunct.

=== Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ III.34, _hēdyosmon_) treats mint as a digestive, antispasmodic, headache and joint-pain remedy, with use as both internal medication and topical aromatic. *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XX) gives extensive uses including as carminative, antidote, and aphrodisiac. *Galen* classes mint as warming and drying, with stomachic and uterine effects. *Apicius* (_De Re Coquinaria_) uses mint heavily in Roman cookery.
- *Mediterranean traditional cuisine:* mint persists in Italian and Greek cooking (Italian _pinzimonio_, Greek lamb-with-mint, _tzatziki_ in some preparations); *spearmint with lamb* is a Roman → modern preserved combination.
- *Medieval European pharmacy:* mint persists in monastic pharmacy (Hildegard, the Salernitan school) as a digestive and brain-tonic; *Aqua menthae* (mint water) and *Aqua menthae piperitae* (peppermint water) become canonical pharmaceutical waters in early modern pharmacopoeias.
- *Early modern peppermint hybridization:* the *sterile hybrid _M. × piperita_* was recognized as a distinct horticultural form in *17th-c. England* and became the basis of the modern peppermint pharmacy register; *Mitcham (Surrey) peppermint* was the classical commercial source, with the village name preserved in older British pharmacopoeial nomenclature.
- *Modern Western herbal:* *peppermint oil* is one of the most-studied and most-used modern herbal medicinal extracts; *enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules* are the standard pharmacognosy for *irritable bowel syndrome* — supported by *multiple high-quality RCTs and Cochrane reviews*. Topical menthol preparations (Tiger Balm, Vicks VapoRub, Bengay, peppermint oil dilutions) for headache, muscle pain, congestion. *Peppermint chewing gum and confectionery* — a major 19th–21st-c. commercial category.

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. Tibb-formulary use of naʿnāʿ is extensively elaborated: mint water, mint syrup, daily mint tea (in regional Tibb-derived practice), mint with yogurt, *tabbūleh* and *fattūsh*, lemon-mint cooling preparations.

=== Biblical / Jewish-Christian register

- *Matthew 23:23, Luke 11:42* — *the canonical New Testament mint reference*: "you tithe mint and dill and cumin and have neglected the weightier matters of the law" — Jesus's critique of Pharisaical legalism, using mint as one of the example kitchen-garden herbs subject to tithing. The passage is *the principal Christian biblical attestation* of mint.
- *Mishnaic-Talmudic herb-tithing discussions* — mint among the kitchen-garden herbs subject to second-tithe (_maʿaser sheni_) under classical Pharisaic / rabbinic practice; preserved in modern Jewish agricultural-law literature.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

The genus _Mentha_ is *naturally distributed across temperate Eurasia and North Africa*, with the principal species adapted to *cool-to-warm-temperate, moist soil, partial sun* conditions. Wild mint species favor *stream banks, wet meadows, and seasonally moist soils* — the genus is *not* drought-adapted. Modern cultivation is global; the principal industrial menthol production is in India, China, and Brazil. The *Mediterranean* and *Levantine* warm-dry-summer / cool-wet-winter climate is the classical home of cultivated mint; the *British Mitcham peppermint* tradition adapts the herb to cool-damp Atlantic. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean and Levantine (the canonical Tibb / Greco-Roman climate)

The classical home climate of cultivated mint. *Daily fresh-mint use* in Levantine cuisine — *tabbūleh, fattūsh, ayran, mint-yogurt* — is the canonical climatically-apt preparation: the *warming Tibb mizāj* is paradoxically used in *cooling-sensory compound preparations* (mint with lemon, mint with yogurt, mint with cucumber) to produce *summer-cooling Pitta-pacifying* dishes. The Egyptian and Levantine *naʿnāʿ infusion* is taken hot in winter (warming-tonic) and as the basis of cold-syrup-mint preparations in summer (cooling-sensory).

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (East European, Russian, Central Asian)

Mint is cultivated for kitchen use; modest classical register; modern use is daily.

=== Cool-damp Atlantic (British Mitcham peppermint tradition)

The *Mitcham (Surrey, southwest London) peppermint* tradition is the classical British commercial register — 17th–19th c. peak production, displaced by 20th-c. North American (Pacific Northwest) and Indian-Chinese-Brazilian industrial menthol. The cool-damp-Atlantic climate suits peppermint cultivation; the resulting essential oil is *high-menthol* by climate-and-soil influence.

=== Hot-humid tropical and continental subtropical (modern industrial cultivation)

The Indian (Uttar Pradesh, Punjab), Chinese (Anhui, Jiangsu), and Brazilian industrial menthol-mint farms produce most modern commercial menthol. The cultivation is *post-classical extension*.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern globalized mint register has spread mint use across all climates and constitutional types; the *warming-aromatic-digestive Tibb register* and the *cooling-surface-releasing Chinese register* coexist with modern uses (peppermint oil for IBS, menthol confectionery, mojitos, modern Western mint tea, oral hygiene). The classical Tibb pairing of *mint with cooling adjuvants (lemon, yogurt, cucumber) in hot weather* preserves a refined climatic-constitutional logic that the modern flat "mint is cool" register loses. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the warm-dry Mediterranean and Levantine profiles.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents of peppermint (_Mentha × piperita_) essential oil:*
- *Menthol* — 35–55%; *the principal compound*; *TRPM8 cold-receptor agonist* — produces the *sensory cooling effect without temperature decrease* (the mechanism that distinguishes mint from any other classical aromatic); anti-spasmodic on smooth muscle; mild analgesic and counter-irritant when topical.
- *Menthone* — 14–32%; aromatic ketone; supports the menthol effect.
- *Menthyl acetate* — 3–10%; contributes to fresh-aromatic character.
- *Neomenthol*, *isomenthone*, *1,8-cineole*, *limonene*, *β-pinene*, *β-caryophyllene*.
- *Trace pulegone* — typically under 5% in peppermint; high concentrations of pulegone are characteristic of pennyroyal (see below).
- *Spearmint (_M. spicata_) essential oil* — dominated by *carvone* (50–70%) and *limonene*; very low in menthol; much sweeter flavor; *not interchangeable with peppermint pharmacologically*.
- *Pennyroyal (_M. pulegium_) essential oil* — *60–80% pulegone*; *toxic*.
- *Rosmarinic acid*, *flavonoids* (eriocitrin, hesperidin, luteolin), *phenolic acids* — antioxidant and anti-inflammatory.#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP _Monographs_, _Menthae piperitae folium_ and _Menthae piperitae aetheroleum_; pulegone toxicology in standard forensic-medicine literature.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Peppermint oil for IBS — the headline modern action.* *Enteric-coated peppermint oil capsules* (typically 0.2 mL three times daily) have *robust evidence* for symptomatic improvement in *irritable bowel syndrome*: multiple high-quality RCTs, *Cochrane Review*, multiple meta-analyses — *one of the better-supported herbal evidence-based applications in modern medicine*. The mechanism: menthol's *L-type calcium channel blockade* on intestinal smooth muscle produces antispasmodic effect; the enteric coating delivers the oil past the stomach (where it would relax the lower esophageal sphincter and worsen GERD) to the intestine. AGA and ACG IBS guidelines include peppermint oil as a recognized intervention.
- *Topical menthol for headache* — modest clinical evidence for migraine and tension headache from topical menthol; the TRPM8-mediated cooling and counter-irritant effect.
- *Carminative and antispasmodic (general)* — well-supported preclinical and clinical evidence.
- *Antimicrobial* — broad-spectrum activity in vitro (Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, some fungi); clinical relevance modest.
- *Anti-nausea* — peppermint aromatherapy modestly reduces nausea in postoperative and chemotherapy contexts; clinical evidence modest-to-favorable.
- *GERD aggravation* — peppermint *relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter*; in GERD patients, *non-enteric-coated peppermint preparations should be avoided* — a clinically significant caveat.
- *Pulegone hepatotoxicity (pennyroyal-specific)* — *Mentha pulegium* essential oil is hepatotoxic via pulegone metabolism by CYP450 to reactive intermediates (menthofuran and beyond) that deplete hepatic glutathione and cause centrilobular necrosis. *Documented fatal cases* from abortifacient use; pennyroyal oil *should not be ingested in any meaningful dose*. *This is one of the most dangerous of all common essential oils*. *Peppermint and spearmint are not hepatotoxic at normal doses* — the safety concern is *species-specific to pennyroyal* and must be flagged.
- *Mild estrogenic and anti-androgenic activity* — spearmint preparations have shown *modest anti-androgenic effect* in clinical studies of women with PCOS (mild reduction in androgens, modest reduction in hirsutism with daily spearmint tea consumption) — an emerging modern application with some clinical support.
- *Lactation reduction* — peppermint and spearmint preparations are reported to reduce milk production in nursing mothers; modern lactation literature supports this; relevant for those weaning.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register (fresh and dried mint in cooking, tea, oral hygiene):* unrestricted at normal intake.
- *Peppermint tea:* 1–2 cups per day; up to 3–4 in active digestive complaint use.
- *Peppermint essential oil for IBS:* *enteric-coated capsules* 0.2 mL three times daily (the standard ESCOP / Commission E / clinical-guideline dose).
- *Topical menthol:* dilute (1–5%) in carrier oil for skin; avoid contact with eyes; *avoid in infants* (high-concentration menthol can cause apnea or laryngospasm in young infants — the *Vicks VapoRub on infants caution* is real).
- *Pennyroyal:* *avoid all internal use*; topical use of pennyroyal essential oil cautioned; *do not use pennyroyal as a substitute for peppermint*.
- *Pregnancy:* therapeutic-dose use of *peppermint oil* cautioned (uterine effects); culinary mint use generally safe. *Pennyroyal absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy* (abortifacient and maternal hepatotoxic risk).
- *Nursing mothers seeking to maintain lactation:* avoid heavy daily mint intake.
- *Infants:* avoid high-concentration menthol topicals on infants under 2 years.
- *GERD:* avoid non-enteric-coated peppermint preparations.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* Pennyroyal toxicity is the principal modern safety concern; species identification matters.#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP _Monographs_, _Menthae piperitae folium_ and _Menthae piperitae aetheroleum_; Commission E monographs; WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_; clinical IBS literature (Khanna, Alammar, McKay, _et al._ meta-analyses and Cochrane Review). Pennyroyal toxicity: forensic and clinical-toxicology literature; the species-specific caveat is critical.]

